From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kan Zhu <kanzhu@cs.washington.edu>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 07:05:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/8] feat(alignment): emit VibeSim alignment records, matching
 the vLLM fork

The alignment report compares a measured serving run against a VibeSim
simulation of the same workload. It already reads four record kinds from an
instrumented vLLM; this teaches SGLang to emit the same four, in the same
schema, down to the field names -- one analyzer, two engines, no per-engine
special cases beyond the new `input_adapter` tag.

* `Iteration` (v2), from `Scheduler.run_batch` + `process_batch_result`: the
  per-forward batch geometry a simulator has to reproduce -- `(prefix, extend)`
  pairs for an extend batch, context lengths for a decode batch -- plus a
  scheduler-side cadence that does not need a profiler attached.
* `RequestTiming` (v2), beside each `set_completion_time()`: TTFT split into
  queue wait and first-schedule-to-first-token, the decode span, and TPOT.
* `ApiRequestTiming` (v3), from `TokenizerManager` on finish: the frontend span
  the client waits on, which the scheduler never sees.
* `ExpertLoad` (v2): builder only for now, not yet wired to EPLB.

SGLang turned out to be most of the way there already, which shaped the work
more than anything else:

* `ScheduleBatch.forward_iter` already is a dispatch-order iteration index, so
  unlike the vLLM fork there was no field to add -- only a geometry snapshot
  hung beside it.
* `ReqTimeStats` is already split into `APIServerReqTimeStats` and
  `SchedulerReqTimeStats`, which is exactly the two-clock-domain split the vLLM
  fork had to build. Each record stays inside one domain and the two join by
  rid, so neither engine's numbers depend on cross-process clock calibration.

Two details worth keeping in mind when reading it:

* The geometry is snapshotted at dispatch, not at result-processing time: the
  decode path advances `seq_lens` during the forward, and in overlap mode the
  next batch is dispatched before the previous result is processed. That is
  also why it hangs off the batch rather than the scheduler.
* `iteration_profile_method` names spans `sglang_iteration(N): <stage>` to match
  the vLLM fork's `vllm_iteration(N)`, but it can only decorate methods where
  `forward_iter` is already assigned. `run_batch` assigns it, so the forward
  keeps SGLang's own `scheduler.run_batch` span and the iteration record's
  `observed_start/end_monotonic_ns` is what windows it on a timeline.

All off behind `SGLANG_ENABLE_VIBESIM_ALIGNMENT` (default false): the iteration
record is one log line per forward pass.

The record arithmetic is pure functions over plain numbers, unit tested with no
GPU, scheduler, or server (11 cases). They cover the derived properties -- the
TTFT parts summing to the whole, the API segments tiling their span -- and the
negative branches that would otherwise fail silently: a zero-token request
dropped from the pairs, a one-token request reporting null TPOT rather than
zero, and an out-of-order or unstamped timestamp producing no row at all.

